        {"after": "# Chromosome theory of inheritance",
         "md": "Mendel's factors were invisible abstractions in 1866. Forty years later, two young researchers — **Walter Sutton** (USA) and **Theodor Boveri** (Germany), working independently in 1902–1903 — pointed out that Mendel's factors behave *exactly* like chromosomes do during meiosis. This is the **chromosome theory of inheritance (Sutton–Boveri theory)**: genes are carried on chromosomes. Thomas Hunt Morgan's white-eyed *Drosophila* mutant (1910) and Calvin Bridges' nondisjunction proof (1916) turned the theory into fact."},
